// code is in the tarball ubel-llvm-ztail-14.tar.gz. Two anchored edits in docs/LLVM_POC.md (Log, Findings). Each
// anchor occurs exactly once and none is a whole line. This overwrites the earlier patch.mdix, which is right: there
// is only ever one (the sema patch that was in it is applied on master).

@CONFIG(
  version    -> "1.0.0",
  author     -> "MidManStudio",
  features   -> "quickfuncs,data",
  debug_mode -> "off"
)

@QUICKFUNCS(
  ~patch_replace_text<object>(_path, _anchor, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "replace_text", anchor = _anchor, content = _content }
  }
)

@DATA(
  patch_files::
    patch_replace_text("docs/LLVM_POC.md", "- 2026-11-20 (chat 9): **M3b, `for` over a list, `contains`, printing lists, and optionals (`pop`, `first`, `last`).**", "- 2026-11-21 (chat 9): **Tail values, and the first cross-language benchmarks.** Writing binary-trees found that a function ending in an `if` or a `match` compiled and then stopped natively (see Findings); fixed in `lower_body` with example 54, `u_tail_if_no_else`, `u_tail_elif_no_else` and one integration test. 9 hand mutants of the fix: all caught after two rounds (a second `elif` fixture, and cases for branches that mix a value and a `return`). New: `crates/ubel_llvm/langbench/` (n-body, spectral-norm, fannkuch-redux and binary-trees in Ubel, C, Rust and C#, with `run.sh`, `timeit.c` and `LANGBENCH.md`, which has the method and the first numbers: on a shared one-core VM, best of 5, Ubel is 1.03x to 1.38x C, geometric mean 1.19x; Rust 0.97x, C# 1.07x). The sema patch that resolves inference variables in expr_types is applied on master; it moved 5 of the 9 fixtures that were refused for an untyped `List.new()` on to their next feature (4 remain, see Findings). The phone reports `armv8l`, and the `ubel-android-armv7` artifact ran fine on it. Checked on a fresh clone of master `687ec29` with the real applier: the cargo tests pass (`ubel_llvm` 6 + 19, core 213, CLI 10 + 14), `run_ubel_build.sh` with the committed tables passes (68 supported programs, 56 intentional refusals, 31 of the 199 `ok_` fixtures build, 168 are refused), `run_equiv.sh` 131 passed, 0 failed at 64-bit and at -m32, and `langbench` prints the expected output in all four languages on the small inputs. Not verified: a real runner, the phone with these programs, macOS, `lsp` and the other crates that need a newer Rust than the 1.85 used here, C# beyond .NET 8 on Linux. Delivery note: the replacements applier matches a tarball member to a manifest target by file name, so a member called `README.md` overwrites the repo's root `README.md`; the benchmark doc is `LANGBENCH.md` for that reason.\n- 2026-11-20 (chat 9): **M3b, `for` over a list, `contains`, printing lists, and optionals (`pop`, `first`, `last`).**")
    patch_replace_text("docs/LLVM_POC.md", "prints the message to stderr, which the suite does not compare.\n\n### Toolchain notes", "prints the message to stderr, which the suite does not compare.\n- (chat 9) A function whose last statement is an `if` or a `match` is that function's value in the interpreter (`fn abs(x: int) int { if x < 0 { 0 - x } else { x } }`), but the emitter compiled such functions and the program then stopped with \"a function with a return type reached its end without returning\": a silent miscompile that no example showed, because they all use an explicit `return`. Found by writing binary-trees. Fixed in `lower_body`: a tail `match` gives the value (arms that `return` keep working); a tail `if` is a value when it has an `else` and every branch ends in an expression, in another such `if`, or in a `return`, with at least one value (so `if x > 0 { 1 } else { return 2 }` works); a tail `if` without `else` whose branch gives a value is refused (`u_tail_if_no_else`, `u_tail_elif_no_else`: sema accepts it and the interpreter returns the value on that path only). Example `54_tail_value`. Not covered: a block-bodied closure whose result type is still open keeps the old behaviour.\n- (chat 9) Sema learns no element type from `push`. The patch that resolves inference variables fixes `let xs: List<int> = List.new()`, but four fixtures write `let items = List.new()` and then `items.push(1)`; the element variable stays unbound after the push, so they are still refused (\"`List.new()` where the element type is not known here\"). Two ways out: sema binds the element type from the pushed value (this changes what sema accepts, for example a list that is pushed an int and then a string), or the emitter infers it from the first push (no language change). Open.\n- (chat 9) The native backend refuses an allocation in a `@tier(mid)` function that is not itself inside a `with arena` (\"where it allocates is not decided yet\"). Sema and the interpreter accept the arena version of binary-trees (depth 10 prints 2047), so this is a design decision about how a MID callee finds its caller's arena, not a missing feature. It blocks the tier comparison in `langbench` (HIGH against MID against LOW on the same program), which is the number that tests Ubel's central claim. Open.\n\n### Toolchain notes")
)
